<h2>Common Tornado Claim Problems</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Incomplete scopes.</strong> Estimates written quickly during a catastrophe response may miss interior damage, framing repairs, or code items.</li>
<li><strong>Contents underestimated.</strong> Personal property inventories take time, and depreciation can sharply reduce actual cash value payments.</li>
<li><strong>ALE disputes.</strong> Questions about how long living expenses will be paid or what is reasonable.</li>
<li><strong>Code and permit costs.</strong> Rebuilding in Dallas requires permits through the City of Dallas Development Services Department, and suburbs like Garland and Rowlett have their own offices. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation holdback.</strong> Under replacement cost policies, part of the payment may be held until repairs are finished.</li>
</ul>
<p>When the insurer&rsquo;s numbers fall short, an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> or <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement</a> may help close the gap.</p>

<h2>Personal Property After a Tornado</h2>
<p>Contents claims are often the most time-consuming part of a tornado loss. Many policies ask for a detailed inventory listing each item, its age, and its replacement cost. Photos and video of each room, purchase records, bank and credit card statements, and online order histories can help rebuild the list. Under replacement cost contents coverage, insurers often pay depreciated value first and the rest after items are replaced. Special limits may apply to jewelry, firearms, collectibles, and business property kept at home, so check those sections before you finalize the list.</p>

<h2>Claim Timelines After a Declared Disaster</h2>
<p>Texas Prompt Payment of Claims rules give insurers set windows to acknowledge a claim, request information, and accept or reject it. After a weather-related catastrophe or major natural disaster declared by the Texas insurance commissioner, those deadlines are extended by 15 days. Large tornado events can also stretch adjuster availability. Keep a written log of every call, inspection, and document request so delays are easy to show. If your claim has stalled, see <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a>.</p>

<h2>How Dallas Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
<p>Dallas Public Adjuster is a service of Rise Public Adjusting LLC, licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance. On a tornado loss we can document the building and contents, prepare a detailed estimate, track ALE and emergency expenses, and communicate with your insurer so you can focus on your family or business. We serve Dallas, Dallas County, and surrounding North Texas communities. We cannot guarantee an outcome. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>First Steps After a Tornado</h2>
<ol>
<li>Make sure everyone is safe. Stay away from downed lines and report gas odors to Atmos Energy.</li>
<li>Photograph and video the damage before cleanup, if it is safe.</li>
<li>Secure the property with tarps and board-up, and keep receipts.</li>
<li>Report the claim and ask about ALE if you cannot stay in the home.</li>
<li>Start a written list of damaged contents while memory is fresh.</li>
</ol>
<p>For legal questions about coverage disputes or deadlines to sue, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
